    </track></source></video></div>
</div>


<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Much of the student discussion will have happened in small groups. Focus the whole-class conversation on whether the quadratic formula works for solving all equations and when it might be a preferred method. Ask students:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> "Look at the list of equations and the work you did to solve them. Do certain equations lend themselves to certain methods of solving? Why?" (We can see the factors of some expressions right away, so rewriting it in factored form would be the quickest method to solve the equations. Other expressions cannot be easily rewritten in factored form but can be easily transformed into perfect squares. For example, when the coefficient of \(x^2\) is 1, the coefficient of the linear term is an even number, and the constant term is also a whole number, completing the square is straightforward. When the squared term has a coefficient other than 1, the other two methods are less practical, so the quadratic formula may be the quickest approach.) </item>
  <item> "Why do you think the quadratic formula is useful only when the \(a\) in \(ax^2+bx+c=0\) is not 0?" (If \(a\) is 0, the expression is no longer quadratic because the squared term disappears, leaving only \(bx+c\), which is linear.) </item>
</list>
<para>Select students who used the quadratic formula to solve the last few equations to explain their solutions and display their work for all to see. Discuss any challenges or disagreements in using the formula.</para>
<para>Tell students that they will use the formula to solve other equations and find out more about its merits and how it compares to other methods of solving.</para>
<para>Watch for algebraic errors with negatives when a value is substituted in for \(b\) when it is made negative and also squared in the formula.</para>
<title>9.6.3: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Solve \(2x^2-6x+3=0\) using the quadratic formula.</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">ANSWERS</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">FEEDBACK</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x=\frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{12}}{4}\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: Remember the formula begins with \(-b\). The correct answer is \(x=\frac{6 \pm \sqrt{12}}{4}\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x=\frac{6 \pm \sqrt{12}}{4}\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That's correct! Check yourself: Substitute \(a = 2\), \(b = -6\), and \(c = 3\) into the quadratic formula then simplify.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x=\frac{6 \pm \sqrt{60}}{4}\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: Be sure to subtract \(4ac\) from \(b^2\), not add. The correct answer is \(x=\frac{6 \pm \sqrt{12}}{4}\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(x=\frac{6 \pm \sqrt{-12}}{4}\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: Remember, when you square a negative number, like in \((-6)^2\), it equals a positive. The correct answer is \(x=\frac{6 \pm \sqrt{12}}{4}\). </para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>9.6.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Solving With the Quadratic Formula</title>

<note>
  <para><emphasis>Quadratic Formula </emphasis></para>
  <para>The solutions to a quadratic equation of the form \(ax^2+bx+c\), where \(a \neq 0\), are given by the formula:</para>
  <para>\(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\)</para>
  
</note>

<para>To use the quadratic formula, we substitute the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) from the standard form into the expression on the right side of the formula. Then we simplify the expression. The result is the pair of solutions to the quadratic equation.</para>
<para>Notice the formula is an equation. Make sure you use both sides of the equation.</para>
<para><emphasis>How to Solve a Quadratic Equation Using the Quadratic Formula</emphasis></para>
<para>Solve by using the quadratic formula: \(2x^2+9x-5=0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the quadratic equation in standard form.  
  This equation is in standard form. 
  \({\style{color:red}a}{\style{color:red}x}^{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}b}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}c}{\style{color:red}=}{\style{color:red}0}\) 
  \(2x^2+9x-5=0\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) values. 
  \( a=2\), \(b=9\), \(c=-5\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the quadratic formula.  
  \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Then substitute in the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\). 
  \({\style{color:blue}a}{\style{color:blue}=}{\style{color:blue}2}\), \({\style{color:red}b}{\style{color:red}=}{\style{color:red}9}\), \({\style{color:green}c}{\style{color:green}=}{\style{color:green}-}{\style{color:green}5}\) 
  \(x=\frac{-9 \pm \sqrt{9^2- 4 \cdot 2 \cdot (-5)}}{2 \cdot 2}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Simplify the fraction and solve for \(x\). 
  \(x=\frac{-9 \pm \sqrt{81-(-40)}}{4}\) 
  \(x=\frac{-9 \pm \sqrt{121}}{4}\) 
  \(x=\frac{-9 +11}{4}\)   \(x=\frac{-9-11}{4}\)
  \(x=\frac{2}{4}\)           \(x=\frac{-20}{4}\)
  \(x=\frac{1}{2}\)           \(x=-5\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Check the solutions. 
  Put each answer in the original equation to check. 
  Substitute \(x=\frac{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}2}\). </para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l} 2\left(\frac{{\style{color:red}1}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\right)^{2}+9 \cdot \frac{{\style{color:red}1}}{{\style{color:red}2}}-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ 2\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)+9 \cdot \frac{1}{2}-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ \frac{2}{4}+\frac{9}{2}-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ \frac{1}{2}+\frac{9}{2}-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ \frac{10}{2}-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ 5-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ 0=0 \text{ ✔} \end{array}\)</para>
<para>Substitute \(x={\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}\).</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l} 2({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5})^{2}+9({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5})-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ 2(25)-45-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ 50-45-5 \stackrel{?}{=} 0 \\ 0=0 \text{ ✔} \end{array}\)</para>
<title>Try It: Solving With the Quadratic Formula</title>
<para>Solve \(2x^2+10x+11=0\) using the quadratic formula.</para>

<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:</para>
<para>Solve by using the quadratic formula: \(2x^2+10x+11=0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - This equation is in standard form. 
  \({\style{color:red}a}{\style{color:red}x}^{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}b}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}c}{\style{color:red}=}{\style{color:red}0}\) 
  \(2x^2+10x+11=0\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\). 
  \(a=2\), \(b=10\), \(c=11\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the quadratic formula. 
  \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Then substitute in the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\). 
  Substitute in: 
  \({\style{color:blue}a}{\style{color:blue}=}{\style{color:blue}2}\), \({\style{color:red}b}{\style{color:red}=}{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}\), \({\style{color:green}c}{\style{color:green}=}{\style{color:green}1}{\style{color:green}1}\)</para>
<para>\(x=\frac{-10 \pm \sqrt{10^2- 4 \cdot 2 \cdot (11)}}{2 \cdot 2}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Simplify. 
  \(x=\frac{-10 \pm \sqrt{100-88}}{4}\) 
  \(x=\frac{-10 \pm \sqrt{12}}{4}\) </para>
<para>Simplify the radical expression. 
  \(x=\frac{-10 \pm 2 \sqrt{3}}{4}\) </para>
<para>Factor out the common factor in the numerator.
  \(x=\frac{2(-5 \pm \sqrt{3})}{4}\) </para>
<para>Remove the common factors.
  \(x=\frac{-5 \pm \sqrt{3}}{2}\) </para>
<para>Rewrite to show two solutions.
  \(x=\frac{-5 + \sqrt{3}}{2}\), \(x=\frac{-5 - \sqrt{3}}{2}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 </emphasis>
  Check the solutions. 
  Put each answer in the original equation to check. 
  Substitute \(x=\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red} +} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\). </para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l} 2\left[\left(\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}}{{\style{color:red}2}}{\style{color:red}+}\frac{\sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\right)\left(\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}}{{\style{color:red}2}}{\style{color:red}+}\frac{\sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\right)\right]+10\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red} +} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 2\left[\frac{25}{4}-\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{4}-\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{3}{4}\right]+5(-5+\sqrt{3})+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \left.2\left[\frac{25}{4}-\frac{10 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{3}{4}\right]+(-25)+5 \sqrt{3}\right)+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \frac{25}{2}-\frac{10 \sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{3}{2}-14+5 \sqrt{3}\stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \frac{28}{2}-5 \sqrt{3}-14+5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 14-5 \sqrt{3}-14+5 \sqrt{3}\stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 0=0 \text{ ✔} \end{array}\)</para>
<para>Substitute \(x=\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red} -} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\).</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l} 2\left(\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}-}\sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\right)^{2}+10 \cdot \frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}-}\sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 2\left[\left(\frac{-5}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\left(\frac{-5}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right]+10\left(\frac{-5-\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 2\left[\frac{25}{4}+\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{3}{4}\right]+5(-5-\sqrt{3})+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \left.2\left[\frac{25}{4}+\frac{10 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{3}{4}\right]+(-25)-5 \sqrt{3}\right)+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \frac{25}{2}+\frac{10 \sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{3}{2}-14+5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \frac{28}{2}+5 \sqrt{3}-14-5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 14+5 \sqrt{3}-14-5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 0=0 \text{ ✔} \end{array}\)</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l} 2\left(\frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}-}\sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}\right)^{2}+10 \cdot \frac{{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}-}\sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}}{{\style{color:red}2}}+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 2\left[\left(\frac{-5}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\left(\frac{-5}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\right]+10\left(\frac{-5-\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 2\left[\frac{25}{4}+\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{5 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{3}{4}\right]+5(-5-\sqrt{3})+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \left.2\left[\frac{25}{4}+\frac{10 \sqrt{3}}{4}+\frac{3}{4}\right]+(-25)-5 \sqrt{3}\right)+11 \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \frac{25}{2}+\frac{10 \sqrt{3}}{2}+\frac{3}{2}-14+5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ \frac{28}{2}+5 \sqrt{3}-14-5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 14+5 \sqrt{3}-14-5 \sqrt{3} \stackrel{?}{=} 0\\ 0=0 \text{ ✔} \end{array}\)</para></section>
